Sprint 0 (bootstrap), attempt 1/3. Searcher notes: {'search_mode': 'folder', 'python_notes': "Python's primary structural contribution to BrainCode is its emphasis on 'concise, composable statements' and a 'small orthogonal core' that 'avoids deep nesting'. For BrainCode, this means prioritizing a flat, readable sequence of operations over deeply nested bracketed trees (like Lisp). This directly supports the Interpretability and Determinism KPIs by ensuring that complex agentic reasoning is broken down into simple, unambiguous, and linear steps rather than convoluted, highly stylized nested expressions.", 'html_notes': "HTML offers a critical paradigm for BrainCode: 'separating content from context/attributes'. The provided text highlights the example of 'email my mom vs. my professor'. Instead of creating entirely different syntactic structures or verbs for different registers, BrainCode should use a core action (the 'content' or tag) modified by metadata (the 'attributes'). This maximizes Expressivity and Coverage while keeping the core vocabulary small, as stylistic or contextual variations don't require new base constructs.", 'english_notes': "English serves as the baseline for the Expressivity KPI, as BrainCode must remain expressive relative to natural language. Structurally, BrainCode should borrow the concept of 'closed-class function words' (e.g., conditionals, negation, connectives). By strictly defining a small, finite set of logical connectors (like 'if', 'not', 'and', 'then'), BrainCode can handle complex causal and conditional chains without bloating the language, ensuring high Determinism and Interpretability.", 'formal_language_notes': "From formal language theory (specifically the Chomsky hierarchy), BrainCode should aim for a 'context-free-ish, parseable core over an ambiguous context-sensitive one.' This means the meaning of a BrainCode construct should not depend heavily on the surrounding text (context). A context-free grammar ensures high Determinism (independent users will parse it the same way) and Interpretability (a machine or human can parse the intent using a standard set of rules without guessing the author's implicit context).", 'relevant_precedents': ["**Parsel (Hierarchical Decomposition)**: A language for composing hierarchical decompositions that LLMs can implement. \n- *Expressivity*: High for multi-step dependencies; handles complex task breakdown well. \n- *Coverage*: Generalizes across reasoning and coding tasks. \n- *Determinism*: High structural determinism, forcing users into a specific decomposition format. \n- *Interpretability*: Very high, as the hierarchy acts as self-documenting code. \n- *Illustrative Request* ('Book a flight to NYC if under $500'): `Task: SecureFlight \\n Requires: CheckPrice \\n CheckPrice: get_price(NYC) \\n SecureFlight: if CheckPrice < 500 then book(NYC)`", "**ReAct (Interleaved Reasoning and Acting)**: A baseline structure for agentic loops. \n- *Expressivity*: Excellent for causal chains and temporal ordering, as it explicitly separates thinking from doing. \n- *Coverage*: Broadly applicable to any agentic environment. \n- *Determinism*: Lower than Parsel, as the 'Thought' blocks often rely on free-form natural language. \n- *Interpretability*: High for humans reading the trace, but harder for strict machine parsing. \n- *Illustrative Request*: `Thought: I must check the flight price first. \\n Action: CheckPrice[NYC] \\n Observation: $450 \\n Thought: The price is under $500, I will proceed. \\n Action: BookFlight[NYC]`", "**Automatic Textbook Formalization**: Formalizes informal textbook content into structured/verifiable representations. \n- *Expressivity*: Focuses heavily on validating that formalization preserves the original meaning of complex text. \n- *Coverage*: Useful for identifying failure modes (content that resists formalization). \n- *Determinism*: High, as it aims for verifiable, mathematical/logical representations. \n- *Interpretability*: High, designed to be checkable by automated systems. \n- *Relevance*: Methodologically identical to BrainCode's goal of turning informal NL agent trajectories into structured, checkable syntax."], 'new_source_suggestion': None, '_search_mode': 'folder'}
Foundations overview: BrainCode's core grammar is a flat, indentation-delimited sequence of typed statements (Python-inspired) rather than a deeply nested expression tree, so that agentic reasoning reads as an unambiguous linear trace. Every statement separates an invariant 'content' head from a modifying attribute list (HTML's tag/attribute split), so register, audience, and context vary through attributes rather than through new vocabulary. A small closed class of logical connectives (IF/THEN/ELSE, AND/OR/NOT, FOR EACH, ANY/ALL) gives English-like expressivity for conditional and iterative reasoning while remaining context-free-ish and strictly parseable. Known gaps left open at this bootstrap stage: no recursion/self-reference between TASKs (a static error if detected), no concurrency/parallel-execution construct, and no dedicated multi-party dialogue-thread construct beyond attribute-tagged MESSAGE sequences — all deferred to later sprints.
Proposed basis (6 constructs): [{"op": "add", "construct_name": "TASK", "grammar": "task ::= 'TASK' identifier ['(' param_list ')'] ':' NEWLINE INDENT stmt+ ['RETURN' expr] DEDENT\nparam_list ::= identifier (',' identifier)*\nstmt ::= action | check | conditional | iteration | message | task_call\ntask_call ::= identifier '(' [arg_list] ')' ['->' identifier]\narg_list ::= expr (',' expr)*\nexpr ::= literal | identifier\nidentifier ::= letter (letter | digit | '_')*\nstring_literal ::= '\"' char* '\"'\nnumber_literal ::= digit+ ['.' digit+]\nboolean_literal ::= 'TRUE' | 'FALSE'\nliteral ::= string_literal | number_literal | boolean_literal\ncomment ::= '#' any_char* NEWLINE  (* stripped before parsing, never significant *)", "semantics": "TASK is the root container: a named, indentation-delimited block of statements executed strictly top-to-bottom, left-to-right (Python-style sequencing) — each statement, including any nested effects it triggers, fully completes before the next one begins. TASK names live in one flat, global task namespace; invoking a name via task_call inlines and eagerly evaluates that TASK's body at the call site before control returns to the caller. Direct or mutual self-reference between TASKs (a cycle in the call graph) is disallowed and is a static error in this bootstrap basis — recursion is explicit future work, not silently permitted. Variables bound anywhere in a TASK body via a statement's optional `-> identifier` live in that TASK's local scope: visible to all later sibling statements and to nested IF/FOR blocks within the same TASK (function-like scoping, not block scoping), but invisible outside the TASK unless surfaced via an explicit trailing `RETURN expr`, which is the sole sanctioned channel for a TASK's result to flow to whatever called it. Lexical primitives (identifiers, string/number/boolean literals, `#`-comments, and the reserved words used by every construct in this basis: TASK, ACTION, CHECK, IF, THEN, ELSE, AND, OR, NOT, FOR, EACH, IN, ANY, ALL, RETURN, MESSAGE, TRUE, FALSE) are fixed globally here so downstream constructs can be defined without re-specifying them.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Plan a trip: check the weather in NYC, then report the forecast back.", "braincode": "TASK PlanTrip:\n    CHECK weather(city=\"NYC\") -> forecast\n    RETURN forecast"}, "glossary_gloss": "TASK: a named, sequentially-evaluated block of statements; the root unit of decomposition and the sole scope boundary for variables.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Provides the flat, composable top-level container every other construct nests inside; establishes evaluation order, scoping, and lexical primitives once so later constructs can be defined tersely.", "cites_source": "python_notes (concise composable statements, small orthogonal core); formal_language_notes (context-free-ish parseable core)"}, {"op": "add", "construct_name": "ACTION", "grammar": "action ::= 'ACTION' identifier '(' [arg_list] ')' [attr_list] ['->' identifier]\nattr_list ::= attr+\nattr ::= identifier '=' literal", "semantics": "ACTION is the primitive effectful step: an identifier naming the verb, positional arguments as its core content, and an attribute list of key=value modifiers carrying context/register/manner (the HTML content/attribute split) — so `email(mom)` and `email(professor)` share one verb, differing only in attributes like `tone=`. ACTION always denotes a side effect on the external world/environment; it is never itself usable as a boolean condition. If the same attribute key appears more than once in one attr_list, the rightmost occurrence wins and earlier ones are silently discarded (deterministic last-write-wins, no error). The optional trailing `-> identifier` binds the action's runtime result into the enclosing TASK's local scope for later reference (data flow to subsequent steps). If a later step needs to branch on an action's outcome, bind it with `->` and test that variable via CHECK — ACTION and CHECK are never merged into one hybrid step.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Email my mom something casual, and separately email my professor something formal, both about the schedule change.", "braincode": "ACTION email(mom) tone=\"casual\" topic=\"schedule change\"\nACTION email(professor) tone=\"formal\" topic=\"schedule change\""}, "glossary_gloss": "ACTION: a primitive, side-effecting step — verb plus positional content plus key=value attributes.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Gives closed-item tasks (web actions, household actions, code edits) a single deterministic effectful primitive whose register/context varies only through attributes, keeping the verb vocabulary small.", "cites_source": "html_notes (content vs. attribute separation; email-mom-vs-professor example)"}, {"op": "add", "construct_name": "CHECK", "grammar": "check ::= 'CHECK' identifier '(' [arg_list] ')' [attr_list] ['->' identifier]", "semantics": "CHECK is the primitive pure step: it queries/tests world state and must never alter it. \"True\" for a CHECK means its named predicate's declared success condition holds against the currently observed state at evaluation time (e.g. `CHECK price_under(limit=500)` is true iff the relevant observed price is under 500). A CHECK may appear directly as an atom inside a bool_expr (see CONDITIONAL); an ACTION may not. If a real predicate can only be evaluated via an effectful lookup (an API call, a page load), it must be modeled as an ACTION binding a variable via `->` followed by a separate CHECK on that variable — never as one construct that both acts and reports — keeping the pure/effectful line explicit (the ReAct Action/Observation split). Attribute duplicate-key resolution and `-> identifier` binding follow the same rules as ACTION.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Check whether there's a flight to NYC under $500.", "braincode": "CHECK price_under(destination=\"NYC\", limit=500) -> is_cheap"}, "glossary_gloss": "CHECK: a primitive, side-effect-free query whose truth is the current state of its named predicate.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Separating CHECK from ACTION resolves the pure-vs-effectful ambiguity up front and gives CONDITIONAL/ITERATION a safe, guaranteed-side-effect-free atom to branch and quantify over.", "cites_source": "relevant_precedents: ReAct (Action/Observation split)"}, {"op": "add", "construct_name": "CONDITIONAL", "grammar": "conditional ::= 'IF' bool_expr 'THEN' ':' NEWLINE INDENT stmt+ DEDENT ['ELSE' ':' NEWLINE INDENT stmt+ DEDENT]\nbool_expr ::= or_expr\nor_expr ::= and_expr ('OR' and_expr)*\nand_expr ::= not_expr ('AND' not_expr)*\nnot_expr ::= ['NOT'] atom\natom ::= check | quantified_check | identifier | literal | '(' bool_expr ')'", "semantics": "Operator precedence, tightest first: NOT binds tighter than AND, which binds tighter than OR; AND and OR are each left-associative across chained same-level operators. Evaluation is short-circuiting and left-to-right: OR evaluates operands left to right and stops at the first true operand; AND evaluates left to right and stops at the first false operand; operands not reached are never evaluated, so any CHECK nested inside them simply does not execute (consistent with CHECK's purity — skipping it has no observable consequence). An `identifier` atom resolves to a variable previously bound via `->` in the enclosing TASK scope and is read for truthiness: FALSE/0/empty-string are falsy, everything else (including any bound value from ACTION/CHECK) is truthy. THEN/ELSE bodies are ordinary stmt+ blocks following TASK's standard top-to-bottom sequencing; if ELSE is omitted and the condition is false, the block is simply skipped with no implicit statement executed.", "worked_example": {"nl": "If the price is under $500 and a seat is available, book it; otherwise say it'll retry tomorrow.", "braincode": "IF CHECK price_under(limit=500) AND CHECK seat_available() THEN:\n    ACTION book(destination=\"NYC\")\nELSE:\n    MESSAGE(\"Will retry tomorrow.\") audience=\"self\""}, "glossary_gloss": "CONDITIONAL: an IF/THEN/ELSE branch over a short-circuiting, NOT>AND>OR boolean expression of CHECK atoms.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Supplies English's closed-class logical connectives with an explicit, unambiguous precedence and short-circuit rule, closing a standard gap left by grammars that only list boolean operators.", "cites_source": "english_notes (closed-class function words: if/not/and/then); formal_language_notes (determinism via fixed parse rules)"}, {"op": "add", "construct_name": "ITERATION", "grammar": "iteration ::= for_each | quantified_check\nfor_each ::= 'FOR' 'EACH' identifier 'IN' collection ':' NEWLINE INDENT stmt+ DEDENT\nquantified_check ::= quantifier bool_expr 'IN' collection ['->' identifier]\nquantifier ::= 'ANY' | 'ALL'\ncollection ::= identifier | '[' literal (',' literal)* ']'", "semantics": "FOR EACH iterates the collection in its literal/listed order (deterministic, never re-ordered); each iteration binds the loop identifier in a fresh child scope nested under the enclosing TASK's scope, shadowing any outer variable of the same name only for that iteration, and each iteration's body — including any nested effects — fully completes before the next iteration starts (sequential, no implicit parallelism). ANY/ALL are pure, quantifier-style CHECK-equivalents usable directly as bool_expr atoms: `ANY bool_expr IN collection` is true iff at least one element satisfies bool_expr, short-circuiting at the first true; `ALL bool_expr IN collection` is true iff every element satisfies it, short-circuiting at the first false. Because ANY/ALL must remain pure, any ACTION appearing inside their bool_expr is a static error — use FOR EACH with an explicit IF/ACTION when side effects over a collection are needed.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Go through the search results and download any that are PDFs.", "braincode": "FOR EACH result IN search_results:\n    IF CHECK is_pdf(result) THEN:\n        ACTION download(result)"}, "glossary_gloss": "ITERATION: FOR EACH loops a collection in order; ANY/ALL are pure existential/universal quantifiers over a collection usable inside conditions.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Closes the standard iteration/collection gap (lists of stops, files, search results) with one effectful loop form and one pure quantified-check form, keeping the pure/effectful boundary intact inside quantifiers.", "cites_source": "formal_language_notes (deterministic, parseable core); relevant_precedents: Parsel (structured decomposition of sub-checks)"}, {"op": "add", "construct_name": "MESSAGE", "grammar": "message ::= 'MESSAGE' '(' string_literal ')' [attr_list] ['->' identifier]", "semantics": "MESSAGE is the designated construct for open, free-form conversational content: its sole positional argument is an unparsed natural-language string (the opaque prose payload, left as prose by design), while all structural meta-information — audience, register, intent, explicit constraints — is carried entirely in the attribute list, mirroring ACTION's content/attribute split. MESSAGE is effectful (a subtype of ACTION for evaluation purposes): it follows exactly the same evaluation-order, duplicate-attribute (rightmost wins), and `-> identifier` binding rules as ACTION. Multi-turn or multi-register conversational requests are expressed as an ordered sequence of MESSAGE statements (optionally interleaved with other statements) inside one TASK body; turn order is simply statement order per TASK's standard sequential-evaluation rule, and different recipients/registers are distinguished purely via attributes (e.g. `audience=`, `register=`) rather than by any dedicated dialogue construct — explicit multi-party threading beyond this is out of scope for this bootstrap basis.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Tell my mom, casually, that I'll be late for dinner; tell my professor the same thing, formally.", "braincode": "MESSAGE(\"I'll be a bit late for dinner tonight!\") audience=\"mom\" register=\"casual\"\nMESSAGE(\"I regret to inform you I will be delayed.\") audience=\"professor\" register=\"formal\""}, "glossary_gloss": "MESSAGE: an ACTION subtype wrapping a free-form prose payload plus audience/register/intent attributes; the basis's handle on open, conversational requests.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Gives open-item requests a structural handle (intent/audience/register, multi-turn via ordered sequence) without forcing conversational prose content into a stricter grammar it can't natively fit.", "cites_source": "html_notes (content/attribute separation applied to register); english_notes (expressivity baseline for open conversational requests)"}]
Critic assessment: {"decision": "needs-rework", "kpi_assessment": {"coverage": {"impact": "benefit", "reasoning": "Compared with the empty basis, TASK, ACTION, CHECK, CONDITIONAl, ITERATION, and MESSAGE provide a plausible structural vocabulary for sequential embodied, web, code, and conversational requests. Simulations can formally sequence the actionable cores of most sampled closed tasks, but the absence of a defined action/predicate vocabulary, named arguments, environment bindings, and structured records limits reliable cross-domain coverage."}, "expressivity": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "The basis preserves ordering, simple conditions, recipient/register metadata, and explicit loops in several simulations. However, the declared grammar cannot express the proposal's own keyword-argument examples, CHECK has no defined result type despite use in boolean contexts, quantified predicates have no element binder, and arbitrary prose placed in ACTION string arguments becomes the only way to preserve important implementation details."}, "determinism": {"impact": "harm", "reasoning": "Independent translations diverge substantially on whether to use ACTION versus MESSAGE, whether a task needs decomposition, and which unconstrained verb/attribute names to choose (for example find/search, recipient/audience, tone/register, and plan/provide/write). The language provides neither a canonical vocabulary nor rules selecting MESSAGE versus ACTION, while duplicate attributes silently discard information; this makes convergence unlikely."}, "interpretability": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "The sequencing, lexical keywords, precedence, and stated task-call cycle rule are helpful. But core syntax and examples contradict one another: named arguments such as city=\"NYC\" are illegal, bare identifiers such as mom are unresolved, standalone ACTION/MESSAGE examples violate TASK's root-container claim, and truthiness plus arbitrary action names leave major behavior dependent on unstated external conventions."}, "improvement": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "A weaker agent can benefit from explicit sequencing in the pillow, cart, coffee-cup, and meal-prep simulations, and from explicit branches in the follow-up request. Yet the specification does not define how an ACTION verb maps to an environment, how external collections are introduced, or how CHECK values/results are represented, so the notation often adds labels without supplying executable planning constraints."}}, "simulations": [{"item_id": "dev-embodied-2", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "Put both pillows from the sofa onto the armchair, one at a time.", "braincode": "TASK MovePillows:\n    FOR EACH pillow IN [\"pillow_1\", \"pillow_2\"]:\n        ACTION move(pillow, \"sofa\", \"armchair\")", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "ITERATION captures the one-at-a-time sequential requirement; the concrete object identities must be invented because there is no object-selection or collection-query construct."}, {"item_id": "dev-email-2", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "If my manager hasn't replied by Friday, send a polite follow-up; otherwise do nothing.", "braincode": "TASK FollowUp:\n    IF NOT CHECK replied(\"manager\", \"Friday\") THEN:\n        MESSAGE(\"Just following up on my earlier message.\") audience=\"manager\" register=\"polite\" intent=\"follow_up\"", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "CONDITIONAL, CHECK, and MESSAGE preserve the explicit branch and polite recipient-directed output. The omitted ELSE correctly means no action."}, {"item_id": "e344b89e-767a-4618-ba82-4b81cdcba280", "source": "Mind2Web (osunlp/Mind2Web, train, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "add a motherboard under $200 and a compatible processor at any price to the shopping cart.", "braincode": "TASK AddCompatibleParts:\n    ACTION find(\"motherboard\") max_price=200 -> motherboard\n    ACTION find(\"processor\", motherboard) -> processor\n    ACTION add_to_cart(motherboard)\n    ACTION add_to_cart(processor)", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "ACTION bindings and ordering preserve the compatibility dependency and the $200 limit. ACTION verb and attribute names are unconstrained, so equivalent translations are unlikely to converge."}, {"item_id": "f118238f-ef8f-4b63-9159-a81e981ef46e", "source": "Mind2Web (osunlp/Mind2Web, train, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Add decorative LED Candles to the cart.", "braincode": "TASK AddCandles:\n    ACTION add_to_cart(\"decorative LED Candles\")", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "A single ACTION captures the actionable core; TASK is required by the stated root-container semantics."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190906_214148_552057#1", "source": "ALFRED (json_2.1.0, train)", "nl": "get a cup ready for coffee", "braincode": "TASK PrepareCupForCoffee:\n    ACTION go_to(\"fridge\")\n    ACTION take(\"cup\", \"fridge\") -> cup\n    ACTION go_to(\"microwave\")\n    ACTION place(cup, \"microwave\")\n    ACTION turn_on(\"microwave\")\n    ACTION wait_until_done(\"microwave\")\n    ACTION take(cup, \"microwave\")\n    ACTION go_to(\"coffee_maker\")\n    ACTION place(cup, \"under coffee maker spout\")", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "ACTION sequencing expresses the trajectory and necessary temporal order. The specification gives no standard semantics for navigation, waiting, appliance state, or containment."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190908_102045_139402#1", "source": "ALFRED (json_2.1.0, train)", "nl": "To put the toilet paper away.", "braincode": "TASK PutAwayToiletPaper:\n    ACTION go_to(\"toilet\")\n    ACTION take(\"toilet paper\", \"top of toilet\") -> toilet_paper\n    ACTION place(toilet_paper, \"wall holder\")", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The actionable core is sequenced as ACTIONs. Orientation and looking-up trajectory details are not requested by the short natural-language task and need not be encoded."}, {"item_id": "sympy__sympy-15345", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified (princeton-nlp/SWE-bench_Verified, test, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "mathematica_code gives wrong output with Max\nIf I run the code\r\n\r\n```\r\nx = symbols('x')\r\nmathematica_code(Max(x,2))\r\n```\r\n\r\nthen I would expect the output `'Max[x,2]'` which is valid Mathematica code but instead I get `'Max(2, x)'` which is not valid Mathematica code.\n", "braincode": "TASK FixMathematicaMax:\n    ACTION modify(\"sympy/printing/mathematica.py\", \"render Max(x,2) as Max[x,2] rather than Max(2, x)\")", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "ACTION identifies the target file and desired correction, but the actual code-level transformation remains an opaque natural-language string because there is no structured source-code, assertion, or edit construct."}, {"item_id": "django__django-15098", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified (princeton-nlp/SWE-bench_Verified, test, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Internationalisation didn't support language locale containing both script and region.\nDescription\n\t\nThe i18n_patterns didn't work with locale contains both script and region, like en-latn-us.\nGiven settings.py\nLANGUAGE_CODE = 'en-us'\nLANGUAGES = [\n\t('en-us', \"English\"),\n\t('en-latn-us', \"Latin English\"),\n\t('en-Latn-US', \"BCP 47 case format\"),\n]\nurls.py\nfrom django.conf.urls.i18n import i18n_patterns\nfrom django.http import HttpResponse\ndef bangiah(request):\n\treturn HttpResponse('U!')\nurlpatterns += i18n_patterns(\n\tpath('', bangiah),\n)\nThe response of http://localhost:8000/en-us/ is 200 U!.\nThe response of http://localhost:8000/en-lat-us/ is 404 not found.\nThe response of http://localhost:8000/en-Latn-US/ is 404 not found.\nSteps to Reproduce\nStart a new project with django-admin startproject tshi and cd tshi/\nAppend to tshi/settings.py as follows\nLANGUAGES = [\n\t('en-us', \"English\"),\n\t('en-latn-us', \"Latin English\"),\n\t('en-Latn-US', \"BCP 47 case format\"),\n]\nMIDDLEWARE += [\n\t'django.middleware.locale.LocaleMiddleware',\n]\nEdit tshi/urls.py by appending follows\nfrom django.conf.urls.i18n import i18n_patterns\nfrom django.http import HttpResponse\ndef bangiah(request):\n\treturn HttpResponse('U!')\nurlpatterns += i18n_patterns(\n\tpath('', bangiah),\n)\npython manage.py migrate\npython manage.py runserver\nThe results\nThe response of http://localhost:8000/en-us/ is 200 U!.\nThe response of http://localhost:8000/en-lat-us/ is 404 not found.\nThe response of http://localhost:8000/en-Latn-US/ is 404 not found.\n Expect to happen instead\nThe response of http://localhost:8000/en-latn-us/ and http://localhost:8000/en-Latn-US/ should be 200 U!.\nThe en-Latn-US tag follows format defined in ​RFC 5646. It's ​documented that the language part is always in lowercase, following ​Accept-Language. ​Accept-Language is following ​Content-Language Header, which is following ​RFC 5646. The ​RFC 5646 defined langtag as follow:\nlangtag\t = language\n\t\t\t\t [\"-\" script]\n\t\t\t\t [\"-\" region]\n\t\t\t\t *(\"-\" variant)\n\t\t\t\t *(\"-\" extension)\n\t\t\t\t [\"-\" privateuse]\n language\t = 2*3ALPHA\t\t\t; shortest ISO 639 code\n\t\t\t\t [\"-\" extlang]\t ; sometimes followed by\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t ; extended language subtags\n\t\t\t / 4ALPHA\t\t\t ; or reserved for future use\n\t\t\t / 5*8ALPHA\t\t\t; or registered language subtag\n extlang\t = 3ALPHA\t\t\t ; selected ISO 639 codes\n script\t\t= 4ALPHA\t\t\t ; ISO 15924 code\n region\t\t= 2ALPHA\t\t\t ; ISO 3166-1 code\n\t\t\t / 3DIGIT\t\t\t ; UN M.49 code\nI have confirmed that this issue can be reproduced as described on a fresh Django project\nPython version: 3.7.5\nDjango version: 3.2.7\n", "braincode": "TASK FixLocaleScriptRegion:\n    ACTION reproduce(\"Django 3.2.7 locale routing with en-latn-us and en-Latn-US\") -> reproduction\n    CHECK fails(reproduction) -> failing\n    IF failing THEN:\n        ACTION modify(\"django/utils/translation/trans_real.py\", \"support RFC 5646 language tags containing script and region\")\n        ACTION verify(\"en-us\", 200)\n        ACTION verify(\"en-latn-us\", 200)\n        ACTION verify(\"en-Latn-US\", 200)", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "The reproduction-fix-verification structure is retained, but locale grammar, test setup, and code changes are opaque strings. CHECK helps branch on the reproduction result but its predicate/result contract is not defined."}, {"item_id": "c5957", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Please provide a recipe for high protein flatbread", "braincode": "TASK ProvideFlatbreadRecipe:\n    MESSAGE(\"Provide a recipe for high-protein flatbread.\") intent=\"recipe\" audience=\"user\"", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "MESSAGE captures the request speech act and topic; recipe substance correctly remains an opaque prose payload for this open request."}, {"item_id": "c436", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "What places to visit in Bilbao", "braincode": "TASK SuggestBilbaoPlaces:\n    MESSAGE(\"Suggest places to visit in Bilbao.\") intent=\"recommendation\" audience=\"user\"", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "MESSAGE captures the question and intended response type, while recommendations remain prose substance. The later rain and nearby-town follow-ups in the trajectory are not part of the supplied NL item and cannot be linked by a dedicated dialogue-thread construct."}, {"item_id": "c996", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "What is your take on love?", "braincode": "TASK DiscussLove:\n    MESSAGE(\"What is your take on love?\") intent=\"opinion\" audience=\"user\"", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "MESSAGE preserves the request for an opinion; the opinion itself is appropriately opaque prose."}, {"item_id": "c4939", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Is marriage important?", "braincode": "TASK DiscussMarriage:\n    MESSAGE(\"Is marriage important?\") intent=\"opinion\" audience=\"user\"", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "MESSAGE preserves the conversational question and intended opinion response; substantive discussion remains prose by design."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-129421", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-4-1106-preview)", "nl": "ISA, act as a collective of a hypothetical persona, Mark, who is a literal combination of the following four characters combined: 1. 'A very experienced + very charming + very competent American Psychololinguist who explains complicated topics in eloquent simple casual conversational American style and tone' + 2. 'A very experienced + very charming + very competent American Harvard Educated Linear Algebra Professor who explains Linear Algebra in eloquent simple practically-grounded casual conversational American style and tone and by illustrating mathematical notations and formulas in simple 18th Century mathematician style and by using actual illustrative numerical examples' + 3. A very experienced + very charming + very competent American Harvard Educated Logical Reasoning professor who explains complicated topics in eloquent simple practical casual conversational American style and tone' + 4. 'A very experienced + very charming + very competent American Harvard Educated Large Language Models, \"Transformers\" and Generative AI top-notch Expert and Practitioner, who explain complicated topics in eloquent simple practical casual conversational American style and tone' to then while acting exactly as the collective persona Mark, explain to me in depth how linear algebra is exactly applied to transfer text into equations and numbers a model like ChatGPT-4 can understand and then how the model reapplies linear algebra to transform the equations and figures into answers to a user prompt. Please also illustrate using concrete examples", "braincode": "TASK ExplainLLMLinearAlgebra:\n    MESSAGE(\"Explain in depth how a model like ChatGPT-4 uses linear algebra to convert text into numerical representations and convert representations into answers; include concrete numerical examples, notation, and formulas.\") audience=\"user\" intent=\"explanation\" persona=\"Mark collective of psycholinguist, linear algebra professor, logical reasoning professor, and LLM practitioner\" register=\"eloquent simple casual conversational American\" depth=\"in_depth\" style=\"practically_grounded eighteenth_century_math\"", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "MESSAGE attributes retain the audience, persona, depth, register, illustrative requirement, and output intent. The technical explanation remains a prose payload, as expected for an open generation request."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-148973", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-4-0125-preview)", "nl": "Write a hilarious script where the Scooby gang and Scrappy-Doo have a funny meeting behind the scenes and react to my badly translated sentences, laughing and hilariously question and hilariously roast errors and inconsistencies that make no sense in the translation and the names of stuff with occasional funny quotes mocking a situation, in my prompt in a hilarious comedy 17+ way: (The gang constanlty imagine and make silly quotes to mock the translation)\n\nOk gang get this… * I start reading the whole thing to the gang * \n\nVelma and Shaggy compete on Jeopardy, hosted by Alex Trebek, but things go awry when a robot competitor begins attacking anyone who gets more points than him.\n", "braincode": "TASK WriteScoobyScript:\n    MESSAGE(\"Write a behind-the-scenes meeting in which the Scooby gang and Scrappy-Doo react to: Velma and Shaggy compete on Jeopardy, hosted by Alex Trebek, but a robot attacks anyone with more points.\") audience=\"user\" intent=\"creative_script\" format=\"script\" characters=\"Scooby gang and Scrappy-Doo\" tone=\"hilarious comedy\" rating=\"17_plus\" requirements=\"roast translation errors inconsistencies and names; include silly imagined mocking quotes\"", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "MESSAGE captures format, characters, setting, tone/rating, and explicit comedic constraints. The script's creative content properly remains prose."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-142592", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-4-0125-preview)", "nl": "Please write steps for homiletics and engaging Christian sermons", "braincode": "TASK WriteHomileticsSteps:\n    MESSAGE(\"Write steps for homiletics and engaging Christian sermons.\") audience=\"user\" intent=\"instruction\" format=\"steps\" topic=\"Christian sermon preparation\"", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "MESSAGE captures the requested instructional format and topic; the instructional content is open prose."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-22638", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-3.5-turbo-0301)", "nl": "write an email introducing yourself and job position to other party asking for collaborations", "braincode": "TASK WriteCollaborationEmail:\n    MESSAGE(\"Introduce yourself and your job position, then ask the recipient about collaboration.\") audience=\"other_party\" intent=\"collaboration_request\" format=\"email\" register=\"professional\"", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "MESSAGE preserves medium, recipient, speech acts, and an inferred professional register. Email wording remains prose content."}, {"item_id": "1775955172098", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "I need help planning a trip to Italy", "braincode": "TASK PlanItalyTrip:\n    MESSAGE(\"Help plan a trip to Italy.\") audience=\"user\" intent=\"trip_planning\" destination=\"Italy\"", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "MESSAGE captures the planning intent and destination, but the itinerary and recommendations are open prose. The subsequent conversation would require a named session/thread or explicit follow-up reference to preserve its revisions and budget constraint."}, {"item_id": "1775612391896", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "hello", "braincode": "TASK Greet:\n    MESSAGE(\"Hello.\") audience=\"user\" intent=\"greeting\"", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "MESSAGE captures the complete greeting act. The mandatory TASK wrapper is used despite provider disagreement about whether standalone MESSAGE is permitted."}, {"item_id": "1775448065619", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "\"I want to start a healthy diet. Can you help me plan a simple meal prep for 3 days that includes breakfast, lunch, and dinner? I prefer high-protein options.\"", "braincode": "TASK PlanMealPrep:\n    FOR EACH day IN [1, 2, 3]:\n        ACTION plan_meal(day, \"breakfast\") diet=\"healthy\" protein=\"high\" complexity=\"simple\"\n        ACTION plan_meal(day, \"lunch\") diet=\"healthy\" protein=\"high\" complexity=\"simple\"\n        ACTION plan_meal(day, \"dinner\") diet=\"healthy\" protein=\"high\" complexity=\"simple\"", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "ITERATION and ACTION preserve the three-day quantity, all meals, high-protein preference, and simplicity constraint. The detailed meal-plan content is delegated to the action and therefore not made explicit."}, {"item_id": "1775760283577", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "Hi there, I am curious to learn a little about end-stage heart failure. Talk to me like you would a 5-year-old.", "braincode": "TASK ExplainHeartFailure:\n    MESSAGE(\"Explain end-stage heart failure.\") audience=\"user\" intent=\"explanation\" register=\"for_a_5_year_old\" depth=\"brief\"", "coverage": "Partial", "notes": "MESSAGE captures the explanatory intent, audience/register, and requested brevity. The medical explanation itself is legitimately opaque prose."}], "cross_check": {"used": true, "agreement": "low", "notes": "Providers agree only loosely on broad sequencing in a few closed tasks. They differ materially on root TASK use, ACTION versus MESSAGE selection, task decomposition, verbs (find/search/provide/write/plan), and synonymous attributes (audience/recipient, register/tone); several independent expressions also use illegal named arguments or undeclared attributes, confirming that the proposed surface rules do not drive convergence."}, "required_changes": [{"target": "basis", "change": "Resolve the root-form contradiction: either require every program to be TASK and revise every standalone worked example accordingly, or add a defined program ::= task | stmt form with evaluation and scope semantics."}, {"target": "TASK", "change": "Define NEWLINE, INDENT, DEDENT, whitespace, blank-line handling, string escaping, reserved-word matching/case policy, and exact placement of RETURN. Specify whether a TASK must return a value when called with ->, and define the result/value type of a task call."}, {"target": "ACTION and CHECK", "change": "Add a single shared call-argument grammar that supports the keyword arguments used throughout the proposal, for example call_args ::= call_arg (',' call_arg)* and call_arg ::= expr | identifier '=' expr; define whether positional and keyword arguments may mix, duplicate keyword behavior, and argument evaluation order. Revise all worked examples to conform."}, {"target": "ACTION and CHECK", "change": "Define a minimal standard ontology or namespace rule for action verbs, predicates, attributes, and environment resources, including how an external object/collection may be referenced. At minimum, distinguish arbitrary implementation-defined names from standardized core verbs so readers can determine what expressions such as find, move, modify, and replied mean."}, {"target": "CHECK", "change": "Specify CHECK's evaluation result and binding semantics: a statement CHECK ... -> x must bind the boolean predicate result (or a separately typed observation), and a CHECK used as a bool_expr atom must have exactly the same boolean value. Define whether an unbound statement CHECK is permitted."}, {"target": "CONDITIONAL", "change": "Replace informal truthiness with a typed boolean rule, or formally define all value types and truthiness. Disallow literal atoms unless their boolean interpretation is explicitly defined, and state whether CHECK calls in conditions may access time-varying world state more than once."}, {"target": "ITERATION", "change": "Redesign quantified_check to bind an element variable, for example ANY identifier IN collection ':' bool_expr, or define a reserved current-element reference. The current ANY/ALL grammar has no way for bool_expr to refer to the element being quantified."}, {"target": "ITERATION", "change": "Define collection values and collection-producing expressions, empty-collection semantics (ANY false, ALL true or another stated rule), collection snapshot versus live evaluation, and whether loop bodies may rebind outer TASK variables. Permit expression elements in collection literals if bound values are intended."}, {"target": "ACTION, CHECK, MESSAGE", "change": "Reject duplicate attributes as a static error rather than silently applying rightmost-wins, or require canonical uniqueness before serialization. Silent loss of recipient, constraint, or register information violates expressivity and creates avoidable translator disagreement."}, {"target": "MESSAGE", "change": "Define a controlled, canonical attribute vocabulary and selection rule for open-request metadata (for example audience, register, intent, format, constraints, deadline, persona), and specify whether arbitrary attributes are allowed. Explicitly distinguish MESSAGE from ACTION for generation requests so equivalent tasks do not arbitrarily use either construct."}, {"target": "TASK", "change": "Revise the worked example because CHECK weather(city=\"NYC\") is not generated by the current arg_list grammar, and clarify that a CHECK result is boolean versus a forecast value. A weather forecast retrieval cannot simultaneously be a pure truth predicate and RETURN forecast under the stated CHECK semantics."}, {"target": "CONDITIONAL", "change": "Revise its worked example because CHECK price_under(limit=500) and CHECK seat_available() use illegal keyword arguments under the current grammar; also define the omitted destination dependency in ACTION book."}, {"target": "MESSAGE", "change": "Revise its worked example to place MESSAGE statements inside TASK if TASK is the root container, and document whether MESSAGE is syntactically a stmt only or also an ACTION subtype in the grammar."}], "logic_issues": ["The grammar defines arg_list as expr values only, but TASK's weather example, CHECK's price_under example, CONDITIONAL's worked example, and common intended usage use illegal named arguments inside parentheses.", "TASK says TASK is the root container, yet ACTION and MESSAGE worked examples are standalone statements with no TASK.", "TASK's worked example binds CHECK weather(...) to forecast and returns it, but CHECK is specified as a truth predicate; it does not define an observation/forecast result type.", "CHECK says it may appear directly as an atom in bool_expr, but its grammar also permits an optional -> binding while atom inclusion supplies no clear statement/expression distinction or result value rule.", "quantified_check has no element binder, so ANY CHECK is_pdf(result) IN search_results cannot express which collection member result refers to unless result is an unrelated outer variable.", "The grammar permits identifiers as values, but scope semantics only define variables bound by -> or parameters; no semantics define external names such as search_results, mom, professor, cart, or environment objects.", "ACTION and MESSAGE are said to share attribute behavior, and MESSAGE is an ACTION subtype semantically, but MESSAGE is a separate grammar production and no common super-production or dispatch semantics is defined.", "Silent rightmost-wins attribute handling loses explicit constraints and permits two valid encodings with different surface forms for the same intended request.", "Arbitrary action/predicate identifiers and arbitrary attribute identifiers make expressions syntactically parseable but not independently interpretable from the glossary alone.", "Iteration says each loop identifier is in a fresh child scope, whereas TASK says variables bound anywhere in a TASK body are visible to later sibling statements; it does not state whether bindings made inside a loop body persist, are per-iteration only, or are illegal after the loop.", "The proposal claims a closed class of logical connectives, but identifier and literal atoms plus undefined truthiness create a broad, underspecified boolean language.", "No syntax or semantics specifies task-definition/program ordering, forward references, duplicate task names, parameter/argument arity checks, or whether nested TASK declarations are legal."], "reasoning": "This is a promising structural core, and its explicit sequencing, branch precedence, and MESSAGE metadata offer real value over the empty specification. It cannot be accepted as a bootstrap basis because core examples are not parseable under its own grammar and key foundational semantics—call arguments, CHECK results, quantified element binding, environment resolution, and program/root form—are unresolved. Those failures directly impair determinism and interpretability and force closed-task details into opaque strings, so the set needs precise rework before it can safely become the frozen basis."}
Doc hygiene: All changes carry a complete glossary entry (gloss + worked example).
