  "foundations_overview": "BrainCode's core is a flat set of TASK-scoped, sequentially-evaluated statements (Python-style indentation, one statement per line, no implicit reordering), inside which a single shared call-argument grammar (positional-then-keyword, HTML's content/attribute split) is reused by every effectful or query construct so keyword arguments like `city=\"NYC\"` are legal everywhere they appear in examples. A strict pure/effectful split (CHECK vs. ACTION/MESSAGE) and a single identifier-resolution rule — a name means something only if it is a TASK parameter, a `->`-bound result, or a loop variable, everything else must be a string literal — give the grammar a context-free-ish, deterministically resolvable reading, closing the ambiguities the Critic found in binding, quantification, and program root form. English's closed-class connectives (IF/AND/OR/NOT) get a fully strict boolean type discipline (no truthy coercion) and live-state re-evaluation semantics, and the basis explicitly documents, rather than silently leaves open, that it has no standardized verb/predicate ontology yet — a deliberate, named gap for later sprints rather than an unstated inconsistency.",
