 "foundations_overview": "BrainCode's core is a flat, indentation-scoped sequence of statements (Python) inside named TASK blocks, with side-effecting verbs carrying a uniform positional-content-plus-keyword-attribute argument shape (HTML's content/attribute split) reused identically by every call-like construct. A small closed class of logical connectives (English's if/not/and/or) gives strictly boolean, short-circuiting control flow, while a normative lexical grammar and a bottom-up static type-inference rule over an acyclic call graph (formal-language determinism) make every binding's value kind checkable before execution. A minimal canonical cross-domain verb ontology on ACTION, and a structured turn-reference/continuation mechanism on MESSAGE, close the two biggest remaining portability gaps without abandoning the six-construct core.",
 "inspiration_summary": "Python motivates TASK's indentation-delimited statement blocks and sequential, function-like local scoping; HTML motivates the single content-plus-attribute call_args grammar shared by ACTION, CHECK, MESSAGE, and task_call; English motivates CONDITIONAL's closed-class if/not/and/or connectives; and formal-language-theory notes motivate the normative lexical grammar and static type-inference rule that make the basis deterministically parseable and checkable rather than context-sensitive.",
 "summary": "Revises all six constructs to add a normative lexical/tokenization grammar, definite-assignment and rebinding rules, a bottom-up static type-inference rule spanning RETURN/task_call/ACTION bindings, a small canonical cross-domain verb ontology on ACTION, and a redefined MESSAGE with delimited keyword arguments plus a structured turn-reference/`reference` continuation mechanism.",
