Sprint 0 (bootstrap), attempt 3/3. Searcher notes: {'search_mode': 'folder', 'python_notes': "Python's primary structural contribution to BrainCode is its emphasis on 'concise, composable statements' and a 'small orthogonal core' that 'avoids deep nesting'. For BrainCode, this means prioritizing a flat, readable sequence of operations over deeply nested bracketed trees (like Lisp). This directly supports the Interpretability and Determinism KPIs by ensuring that complex agentic reasoning is broken down into simple, unambiguous, and linear steps rather than convoluted, highly stylized nested expressions.", 'html_notes': "HTML offers a critical paradigm for BrainCode: 'separating content from context/attributes'. The provided text highlights the example of 'email my mom vs. my professor'. Instead of creating entirely different syntactic structures or verbs for different registers, BrainCode should use a core action (the 'content' or tag) modified by metadata (the 'attributes'). This maximizes Expressivity and Coverage while keeping the core vocabulary small, as stylistic or contextual variations don't require new base constructs.", 'english_notes': "English serves as the baseline for the Expressivity KPI, as BrainCode must remain expressive relative to natural language. Structurally, BrainCode should borrow the concept of 'closed-class function words' (e.g., conditionals, negation, connectives). By strictly defining a small, finite set of logical connectors (like 'if', 'not', 'and', 'then'), BrainCode can handle complex causal and conditional chains without bloating the language, ensuring high Determinism and Interpretability.", 'formal_language_notes': "From formal language theory (specifically the Chomsky hierarchy), BrainCode should aim for a 'context-free-ish, parseable core over an ambiguous context-sensitive one.' This means the meaning of a BrainCode construct should not depend heavily on the surrounding text (context). A context-free grammar ensures high Determinism (independent users will parse it the same way) and Interpretability (a machine or human can parse the intent using a standard set of rules without guessing the author's implicit context).", 'relevant_precedents': ["**Parsel (Hierarchical Decomposition)**: A language for composing hierarchical decompositions that LLMs can implement. \n- *Expressivity*: High for multi-step dependencies; handles complex task breakdown well. \n- *Coverage*: Generalizes across reasoning and coding tasks. \n- *Determinism*: High structural determinism, forcing users into a specific decomposition format. \n- *Interpretability*: Very high, as the hierarchy acts as self-documenting code. \n- *Illustrative Request* ('Book a flight to NYC if under $500'): `Task: SecureFlight \\n Requires: CheckPrice \\n CheckPrice: get_price(NYC) \\n SecureFlight: if CheckPrice < 500 then book(NYC)`", "**ReAct (Interleaved Reasoning and Acting)**: A baseline structure for agentic loops. \n- *Expressivity*: Excellent for causal chains and temporal ordering, as it explicitly separates thinking from doing. \n- *Coverage*: Broadly applicable to any agentic environment. \n- *Determinism*: Lower than Parsel, as the 'Thought' blocks often rely on free-form natural language. \n- *Interpretability*: High for humans reading the trace, but harder for strict machine parsing. \n- *Illustrative Request*: `Thought: I must check the flight price first. \\n Action: CheckPrice[NYC] \\n Observation: $450 \\n Thought: The price is under $500, I will proceed. \\n Action: BookFlight[NYC]`", "**Automatic Textbook Formalization**: Formalizes informal textbook content into structured/verifiable representations. \n- *Expressivity*: Focuses heavily on validating that formalization preserves the original meaning of complex text. \n- *Coverage*: Useful for identifying failure modes (content that resists formalization). \n- *Determinism*: High, as it aims for verifiable, mathematical/logical representations. \n- *Interpretability*: High, designed to be checkable by automated systems. \n- *Relevance*: Methodologically identical to BrainCode's goal of turning informal NL agent trajectories into structured, checkable syntax."], 'new_source_suggestion': None, '_search_mode': 'folder'}
Foundations overview: BrainCode's core is a flat, indentation-scoped sequence of statements (Python) inside named TASK blocks, with side-effecting verbs carrying a uniform positional-content-plus-keyword-attribute argument shape (HTML's content/attribute split) reused identically by every call-like construct. A small closed class of logical connectives (English's if/not/and/or) gives strictly boolean, short-circuiting control flow, while a normative lexical grammar and a bottom-up static type-inference rule over an acyclic call graph (formal-language determinism) make every binding's value kind checkable before execution. A minimal canonical cross-domain verb ontology on ACTION, and a structured turn-reference/continuation mechanism on MESSAGE, close the two biggest remaining portability gaps without abandoning the six-construct core.
Proposed basis (2 constructs): [{"op": "revise", "construct_name": "TASK", "grammar": "program ::= task+\ntask ::= 'TASK' identifier ['(' param_list ')'] ':' NEWLINE INDENT stmt+ [return_stmt] DEDENT\nreturn_stmt ::= 'RETURN' expr NEWLINE\nparam_list ::= identifier (',' identifier)*\nstmt ::= action | check_stmt | conditional | iteration | message | task_call_stmt\ntask_call_stmt ::= task_call NEWLINE\ntask_call ::= identifier '(' [call_args] ')' ['->' identifier]\n\n(* shared call-argument grammar, reused verbatim by ACTION, CHECK, task_call, and MESSAGE's kw_args *)\ncall_args ::= call_arg (',' call_arg)*\ncall_arg  ::= pos_arg | kw_arg\npos_arg   ::= expr\nkw_arg    ::= identifier '=' expr\n(* all pos_arg entries in one call_args must precede all kw_arg entries; a duplicate kw_arg key within one call_args is a static parse-time error *)\n\nexpr ::= literal | identifier | list_literal\nlist_literal ::= '[' [expr (',' expr)*] ']'\nliteral ::= string_literal | number_literal | boolean_literal\nidentifier ::= letter (letter | digit | '_')*\nletter ::= 'a'..'z' | 'A'..'Z'\ndigit ::= '0'..'9'\n\n(* --- Lexical layer (normative) --- *)\nstring_literal ::= '\"' str_char* '\"'\nstr_char ::= any_source_char_except('\"', '\\\\', raw_newline) | escape_seq\nescape_seq ::= '\\\\\"' | '\\\\\\\\' | '\\\\n' | '\\\\t'\n(* a raw, unescaped newline inside a string_literal is a lexical error; the string must use the \\n escape instead; an unterminated string_literal (EOF or raw newline reached before a closing '\"') is a lexical error *)\nnumber_literal ::= digit+ ['.' digit+]\nboolean_literal ::= 'TRUE' | 'FALSE'\ncomment ::= '#' any_char_except(newline)* NEWLINE  (* stripped before parsing; never significant; never permitted inside a string_literal *)\n(* Tokenization: the lexer scans left to right, taking the longest match at each position, over these token classes: reserved words, identifier, number_literal, string_literal, boolean_literal, and the single-character punctuation tokens ( ) [ ] , = : plus the two-character token '->' . ASCII space (U+0020) is the only intra-line whitespace; it separates otherwise-adjacent tokens but is itself never a token and carries no meaning beyond separation (one-or-more spaces between tokens is equivalent to exactly one, except inside string_literal where every character, including spaces, is significant verbatim). Two tokens that would otherwise lexically merge (e.g. two adjacent identifiers, or a reserved word immediately followed by an identifier character) MUST be separated by at least one space; this is never optional. A tab character anywhere outside a string_literal (including in indentation) is a lexical error. NEWLINE ends each logical (non-blank, non-comment-only) line; INDENT/DEDENT are generated Python-style by comparing each logical line's leading-space count to its enclosing block's; blank and comment-only lines never affect INDENT/DEDENT. Reserved words (TASK, ACTION, CHECK, IF, THEN, ELSE, AND, OR, NOT, FOR, EACH, IN, ANY, ALL, RETURN, MESSAGE, TRUE, FALSE) are case-sensitive and must appear exactly as written; an identifier may not equal a reserved word.)", "semantics": "A BrainCode program is one or more top-level TASK definitions; TASK bodies never contain a nested `task` (nested TASK declarations are a static error). Every top-level TASK name must be unique; task_call resolves against the flat namespace of all top-level TASK names regardless of textual order (forward references permitted). Exactly one top-level TASK must be uncalled anywhere in the program; that TASK is the entry point. Zero or more-than-one uncalled top-level TASK is a static error. Direct or mutual recursion (a cycle in the static call graph) is a static error — this makes the call graph a DAG, which the type-inference rule below relies on. A task_call's call_args must match the callee's param_list in arity, with keyword names matching declared parameter names exactly. Statements execute strictly top-to-bottom, each fully completing (including nested effects) before the next begins.\n\nBinding, rebinding, and scope: `->` bindings live in the enclosing TASK's local, function-like scope, visible to later sibling statements unless surfaced by RETURN. A `->` target name may never equal a declared TASK parameter name (rebinding a parameter is a static error). A `->` target name that repeats a name already bound earlier via `->` in that exact same scope is permitted and rebinds it — the new value replaces the old for all subsequent statements in that scope, ordinary sequential reassignment.\n\nDefinite assignment across branches and loops: a `->` binding made inside an IF/THEN block or an ELSE block is, by default, block-local and is NOT visible after the conditional's DEDENT (referencing it afterward is a static error). The one exception: if an ELSE block is present and the same name X is bound via `->` in both the THEN and the ELSE block, X is definitely assigned on every path and becomes visible, with ordinary TASK-scope persistence, after the DEDENT (if the two branches infer different kinds for X, that is a static error). Because a FOR EACH body may execute zero times, any `->` binding made inside a FOR EACH body — other than the loop variable itself, which never persists at all (see ITERATION) — is likewise block-local to that body and never visible past the loop's DEDENT, regardless of how many iterations actually ran; a value that must outlive a loop must instead be bound, unconditionally, by a separate statement outside the loop. Accumulating a running value across loop iterations into one post-loop binding is an explicit, named gap for a later sprint.\n\nStatic type inference (RETURN / task_call / identifiers): every expr has a statically determined value kind — string, number, boolean, or list — with two additional special markers: void (no value at all; produced only by certain canonical ACTION verbs, see ACTION) and opaque-unknown (a value whose kind this basis cannot determine). Kinds are computed as: a literal's kind is its literal form; a list_literal's kind is list; an identifier's kind is the kind already established at its binding site: (a) bound from a check_stmt or quantified_stmt → boolean; (b) bound from a task_call → the callee TASK's inferred RETURN-expr kind, computed by applying this same rule inside the callee (well-founded and terminating because the call graph is acyclic); (c) bound from an ACTION whose verb is canonical → that verb's documented return kind; (d) bound from an ACTION whose verb is non-canonical → the kind declared by a `returns=` kw_arg at that call site if present, else opaque-unknown; (e) a TASK parameter → opaque-unknown. A RETURN statement's kind is its expr's kind by this same rule. An identifier of kind void or opaque-unknown may still be passed through, unconstrained, as a positional/keyword argument to another ACTION/CHECK/task_call, but may never be used as a bool_expr atom, an ITERATION collection, or a MESSAGE `reference` target, each of which requires a specific known kind; doing so is a static type error. This is the mechanism that makes CONDITIONAL's requirement — that a task_call's bound result be statically known boolean — checkable: kinds are computed bottom-up, once per TASK, before any caller's bool_expr is checked.\n\nBrainCode recognizes four ordinary value kinds a variable can hold (string, number, boolean, list) plus the two special markers above and MESSAGE's distinct turn-reference kind (see MESSAGE). There is no general structural type checker beyond this kind-inference rule; mismatches are rejected only where a specific construct restricts which kind it accepts. Any identifier appearing in expr/bool_expr/collection position must be a TASK parameter, a name bound via `->` earlier in the same scope (subject to the definite-assignment rules above), or an enclosing loop variable; any other bare identifier is an undefined-reference static error — external world resources, recipients, or objects never explicitly bound must be written as string_literal. expr deliberately excludes calls (no nested ACTION/CHECK/task_call inside an argument expression): to pass a prior step's result as a later argument, bind it with `->` first — this is BrainCode's sole data-flow channel and keeps argument evaluation order (strictly left-to-right, no side effects) unambiguous.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Look up the weather in NYC and return the forecast.", "braincode": "TASK PlanTrip:\n    ACTION get_weather(city=\"NYC\") -> forecast\n    RETURN forecast"}, "glossary_gloss": "TASK: a named, sequentially-evaluated block of statements with function-like scoping, definite-assignment rules for branch/loop-local bindings, and a static kind (string/number/boolean/list/void/opaque-unknown) inferred for every RETURN and bound identifier; a program is one or more top-level TASKs, exactly one of which (the uncalled one) is the entry point.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Adds the normative lexical/escape/tokenization grammar the Critic required, replaces the previously silent binding rules with explicit rebinding/definite-assignment/zero-iteration-loop rules, adds the bottom-up static type-inference function that makes CONDITIONAL's and ITERATION's typed-position claims actually checkable, and corrects the worked example so its NL matches what the code does (retrieve-and-return, not report).", "cites_source": "python_notes (concise composable statements, flat indentation scoping); formal_language_notes (context-free-ish parseable core; static, checkable typing over an ambiguous context-sensitive one)"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "ACTION", "grammar": "action ::= 'ACTION' identifier '(' [call_args] ')' ['->' identifier] NEWLINE\n(* call_args, pos_arg, kw_arg as defined under TASK *)", "semantics": "ACTION is the primitive effectful step: an identifier naming the verb, followed by the shared call_args list — positional args carry core content, keyword args carry contextual modifiers, mirroring HTML's content/attribute split. A duplicate kw_arg key within one ACTION's call_args is a static parse-time error. ACTION's own call expression is never itself usable inline as a bool_expr atom (only check_call/quantified_check are, by the atom production) — but a value that an ACTION *binds* via `->` may later be referenced as a bool_identifier if its statically inferred kind is boolean (see below and TASK's type-inference rule).\n\nCanonical verb ontology (normative core covering the six most common cross-domain effect classes, closing the prior open-vocabulary gap): a translator SHOULD prefer one of these exact verb names, with exactly this argument/return shape, whenever the requested effect and result shape (list vs. single item, void vs. boolean) match a table entry:\n- search(query: string_literal, [source=string_literal], [any additional kw_arg filters]) -> list: finds/looks up a set of matching resources (web results, files, products, flights); returns a list of opaque resource references.\n- filter(collection, criterion: string_literal) -> list: narrows a list to elements matching criterion (criterion remains opaque prose — a filter-predicate sub-grammar is not yet standardized).\n- sort(collection, [by=string_literal], [order=\"ascending\"|\"descending\""}]
